      }
    },
    {
      "id": "the-merry-wives-of-windsor/sceneIII_23.2/p@88681",
      "type": "speech",
      "tag": "p",
      "classes": [
        "drama"
      ],
      "text": "FORD\nHas Page any brains? Hath he any eyes? Hath he any thinking? Sure, they sleep; he hath no use of them. Why, this boy will carry a letter twenty mile as easy as a cannon will shoot point-blank twelve score. He pieces out his wife’s inclination, he gives her folly motion and advantage. And now she’s going to my wife, and Falstaff’s boy with her. A man may hear this shower sing in the wind. And Falstaff’s boy with her! Good plots they are laid, and our revolted wives share damnation together. Well, I will take him, then torture my wife, pluck the borrowed veil of modesty from the so-seeming Mistress Page, divulge Page himself for a secure and wilful Actaeon, and to these violent proceedings all my neighbours shall cry aim. [Clock strikes.] The clock gives me my cue, and my assurance bids me search. There I shall find Falstaff. I shall be rather praised for this than mocked, for it is as positive as the earth is firm that Falstaff is there. I will go.",
      "separatorAfter": "\n\n",
      "spans": [
        {
          "tag": "i",
          "start": 736,
          "end": 749
        }
      ],
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "the-merry-wives-of-windsor/sceneIII_23.2/p@88681/line-1",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 4,
          "sourceLineNumber": null
        },
        {
          "id": "the-merry-wives-of-windsor/sceneIII_23.2/p@88681/line-2",
          "start": 5,
          "end": 966,
          "sourceLineNumber": null
        }
      ],
      "sourceRef": {
        "start": 4132143,
        "end": 4133158,
